1. Go to the project folder
cd /home/cse/24WH1A05D4_Dev/EventRegistrationMicroservices

Check:

pwd

It should show:

/home/cse/24WH1A05D4_Dev/EventRegistrationMicroservices
2. Check that both services are present
ls

You should have:

event-service
registration-service

⚠️ You also have a folder called:

registration-service (1)

Do not add that folder unless you actually need it. Your required service is:

registration-service
3. Check both services
ls event-service

and:

ls registration-service

You should see files such as pom.xml, src, mvnw, etc.

4. Initialize Git

If Git is already initialized, this command is harmless:

git init

5. Configure your Git identity
git config --global user.name "Shrena Reddy"

Use your actual GitHub email:

git config --global user.email "YOUR_GITHUB_EMAIL"

Check:

git config --global --list
6. Create the root .gitignore
nano .gitignore

Put this inside:

target/
*.class
*.log
.classpath
.project
.settings/

Save with:

Ctrl + O → Enter → Ctrl + X

This prevents Maven-generated target files from being pushed.

7. Check Git status
git status
8. Add BOTH services

This is the important command:

git add event-service registration-service .gitignore

Or simply:

git add .

I recommend:

git add .
9. Check what will be committed
git status

You should see files from both:

event-service/...
registration-service/...
.gitignore

You should not see:

target/

files if your .gitignore is working.

10. Create the initial commit
git commit -m "Add event and registration microservices"

11. Use main as the branch
git branch -M main

Check:

git branch

You should see:

* main
12. Remove the incorrect remote

Because you previously added:

github.com

remove it:

git remote remove origin

Check:

git remote -v

It should show nothing.

13. Add your actual GitHub repository

If your GitHub repository is named:

event-registration-microservices

and your GitHub username is:

ShrenaReddy

use:

git remote add origin https://github.com/ShrenaReddy/event-registration-microservices.git
Verify it
git remote -v

You should get:

origin  https://github.com/ShrenaReddy/event-registration-microservices.git (fetch)
origin  https://github.com/ShrenaReddy/event-registration-microservices.git (push)

14. Push BOTH services to GitHub
git push -u origin main

When it asks:

Username for 'https://github.com':

enter:

ShrenaReddy

When it asks:

Password for 'https://github.com':

Do not enter your normal GitHub password.

Enter your GitHub Personal Access Token (PAT).

When you paste the token, the terminal may appear to show nothing. That is normal. Paste it and press Enter.
